/** YD001 quality-pass translations siman 112 */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `Marriage [concern]. The Shach wrote: but regarding the revulsion of gentiles there is no concern, for ordinarily their vessels are not ben yomo; also, pat akum is not forbidden unless a non-Jew baked it. And what he wrote regarding a place where there is no marriage concern — meaning, such as the bread of heretics who have no children — is forbidden, for if this one has no children, that one does; the Sages did not differentiate.`,
    '1#ב': `Grain. For this is an important matter and leads to familiarity. Taz.`,
    '1#ג': `Kings. The Bach challenged this and wrote that there is no doubt that bread of legumes, rice, and millet does not go upon a king's table; rather, the Rosh deals with a cooked dish made from rice and millet, regarding which there is a distinction whether it goes upon a king's table or not. See Turei Chayim, law 11, in the name of Issur VeHeter.`,
    '2#א': `Baker. The Shach wrote: specifically pat akum; but pat Yisrael that a non-Jew baked — [and did not kasher the oven by throwing kisam] — nowhere was the practice to permit it, for it is like bishulei akum; the reason they did not forbid pat akum is that it is a decree the majority of the community cannot abide by; but regarding pat Yisrael that a non-Jew baked, that reason does not apply; therefore it is forbidden.`,
    '2#ב': `Available. In the Mordechai he wrote the reason: because those places that practice leniency did so since the prohibition of pat akum did not spread at the beginning of the decree in those places, for the prohibition of pat akum did not spread throughout all Israel. Accordingly it appears that even pat baal habayit is permitted for that reason; but from the words of Issur VeHeter and the Rama it is clear that even those who practiced leniency regarding pat palter forbid pat baal habayit. Turei Chayim wrote that even those who practice leniency regarding pat akum should be careful on the Ten Days of Repentance; and so explained Orach Chaim, siman 242, seif 2 — see there. However, it appears one should be careful all year round not to buy pat akum from the palter where there is a Jewish palter, unless the pat akum is better — then it is permitted. Shach.`,
    '3#_': `He ordered. The Shach wrote: it is difficult, for the Mechaber holds below, seif 7, that we follow the beginning; how then did he write this law here? Perhaps for that reason they wrote in the name of "some say," or it is possible he holds according to his reasoning to distinguish that when the palter was ordered by him it is worse — end of his words.`,
    '4#_': `To buy. What the Shach challenged here was corrected [by us] in Nachalat Binyamin, that he erred between the word "likach" and "lekach" — see there; investigate. All this is according to the Mechaber, seif 2; but according to the second view in the gloss, it is plainly permitted.`,
    '6#א': `With eggs. Why should we be concerned? If because of blood — the majority of eggs have no blood, as above, siman 66, seif 8. If because of an egg of an impure bird — they are not common among us, as above, siman 86, seif 2. If because of bishulei akum — eggs are forbidden; we establish that flour is primary — end of his words, Shach; and see above, siman 113.`,
    '6#ב': `Sweet kinds. Since if one establishes a meal upon them he recites ha-Motzi, their law is like bread proper. Taz; and specifically those whose batter is [thick] and have the status of bread; and so too in Turei Chayim.`,
    '10#_': `Kashering. Hagahot ShaDa wrote that if one is in doubt whether a Jew kashered the oven or not, it is permitted, for it is a d'rabbanan doubt and we rule leniently; and so too the Rav in Turei Chayim.`,
    '13#_': `He cuts. The Taz and Shach wrote; and what the Mechaber wrote in Orach Chaim, siman 168, that one removes pat akum from the table until after ha-Motzi — there it deals with when no other person is reclining with him; but here, since another Jew is reclining with him, if he does not bless on clean bread there is enmity. And even though it appears there that if only one person is reclining with him there is no enmity — what comparison is there between this and that? — nevertheless, since regarding ha-Motzi one is obligated to bless on the finest, if he does not bless there is great enmity. Alternatively, specifically when both their breads are equal is there no enmity when there is only one [guest]; but when the bread of one who is not careful is cleaner and finer and nevertheless he does not eat it, there is certainly enmity.`,
    '14#א': `Kutach. The Taz wrote that one should not forbid it because of gentile milk, for impure milk does not stand; and if we say there is a slight admixture of milk in it, nevertheless it is nullified in its minority since it is mixed in, and it is not comparable to butter in siman 115 which is not mixed in — end of Rashal's words. To me, the younger, it seems astonishing that milk admixture is certainly not nullified in its minority; proof from the Gemara, chapter Elu Overin, Babylonian kutach, etc.; and also in chapter Ein Ma'amidin it states that one who curdled kemakha — which is kutach — the milk is forbidden. Rather, here it deals with when it was made with supervision that a Jew sees it, only that the non-Jew puts his bread there, for there is no prohibition of pat akum in that — end of his words.`,
    '14#ב': `Moist. Even if it is an important item, these laws apply even to actual bishulei akum — so too in Issur VeHeter and Turei Chayim. And what he wrote, "but it is forbidden to mix it" — Darkei Moshe wrote on this, and it appears to me that nevertheless one may be permitted to eat in one bowl and there is no reason to forbid because of taste that became mixed; they only forbade mixing l'chatchila to eat the bread itself; but regarding taste there is no concern. However, in Hagahot ShaDa he forbids l'chatchila — end of his words. The Shach wrote that this is not necessary; one may say it also deals when one eats the bread itself; and in any case the bowl is a second vessel and he is not cooking.`,
    '15#_': `Prohibitions. The Shach wrote: even gentile butter and the like — in a place where they practice prohibition, a light prohibition is still a prohibition; specifically regarding bread there is enmity, because "by bread shall man live"; but not regarding other foods, for many people have no desire to eat butter, and the like. The Taz wrote that the Beit Yosef proved one may buy gentile leaven and use it to ferment dough, even for one who is careful about pat akum, for the Sages did not decree on pat akum regarding taste like the substance itself; but regarding crumbs that are visible, they are certainly forbidden as long as they were not nullified in the majority — end of his words.`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#א': `Mishnah, Avodah Zarah, daf 35.`,
    '1#ב': `Tur in the name of his father's responsum, the Rosh (and Rabbeinu Yerucham).`,
    '2#א': `Tur in the name of the second view, and according to the Yerushalmi; and so the Rashba and Ran (and Rambam, chapter 17 of Hilchot Ma'achalot Assurot) — Rabbi Yochanan does not disagree; on the contrary, Rabbi Chelbo there, daf 35; and we rule according to both leniently.`,
    '2#ב': `There.`,
    '3#_': `Orach Chayim in the name of the Ra'ah (and Kelbo, and it is in Bedek HaBayit, daf 88, side 2).`,
    '4#_': `There.`,
    '5#א': `Tur in the name of the Rashba in Torat HaBayit (and so the Ran).`,
    '5#ב': `Beit Yosef in the name of the Rashba there in Torat HaBayit; and so is the view of his teacher the Rav R' Yosef.`,
    '6#א': `Tosafot, the Rosh, and the Ran there; and the Rashba in Torat HaBayit.`,
    '6#ב': `There, in seif 5, that there is no concern of bishulei akum in them, for flour is primary; and one need not be concerned about blood in eggs or an egg of an impure bird, for we follow the majority.`,
    '6#ג': `There; and see in the siman after this, seif 3.`,
    '7#_': `The Rosh in a responsum, the Rashba in Torat HaBayit, and the Ran in chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah — from that house of wheat upon which a barrel of yayin nesech fell, that Rav permitted to grind and to sift and to sell to gentiles, there, daf 65.`,
    '8#_': `Sefer Orach Chayim in the name of the Ra'am and Kelbo.`,
    '9#א': `The statement of Ravina: the halachah is this bread, etc. — Avodah Zarah, daf 38.`,
    '9#ב': `Tur in the name of the Rambam, chapter 7 of Hilchot Ma'achalot Assurot.`,
    '10#_': `Mordechai, chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah, according to the incident.`,
    '11#_': `There in the Mordechai in the name of Rabbeinu Meir — from that boat that sank in a swamp, Pesachim, daf 40; and Agudah; and ShaDa; and Semak; and Kol Bo.`,
    '12#א': `There.`,
    '12#ב': `The Agur in the name of ShaDa (and from Maharai; and Issur VeHeter).`,
    '13#_': `Mordechai there in the name of Ravyah — from that in Eruvin, daf 41: if he is clever he enters his boundary, etc. — that they were not stringent regarding d'rabbanan prohibitions, for there is no honor of Heaven that he should again refrain because of a prohibition when he has already transgressed (Agudah and Terumat HaDeshen, siman 32).`,
    '14#_': `Tur in the name of the Geonim and the Rashba in the name of the Rosh.`,
    '15#_': `There in the name of R' Yitzchak in Tosafot, Avodah Zarah, daf 35 — from that they did not decree on mixtures of demai, Chullin, daf 6.`,
    '16#_': `So too in the name of Sefer Agudah, chapter 9 of Chullin.`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Because of marriage concern. Gemara, 35, side 2; and see there, 36, side 2; and in Shabbos, 17, side 2.`,
    '1#ב': `And even in a place, etc. Such as from heretics — for we also say regarding their wine because of their daughters, as above; and see Tosafot there, 29, side 2, s.v. wine, etc.; and one may say, etc.; and there, 57, side 1, a child, son, etc.; and this is what it means "because of their daughters," and there is no difficulty "because of gentiles' daughters."`,
    '1#ג': `And they did not forbid, etc. As written in chapter 6 of Berachot (37, side 1) and first chapter of Challah.`,
    '1#ד': `And also, etc. That is to say, bread has no concern of bishulei akum; and where bread was permitted it is entirely permitted, for it was not in the category of sheluhot; but a thing that is not bread remains in the category of sheluhot. And even when bread was permitted, only a thing that is bread was permitted — and see in the gloss.`,
    '2#א': `There are places, etc. This entire seif is the Rambam's language, and as written in the Yerushalmi, chapter Kol Sha'ah: gentile leaven, etc. — Mishnah: in a place where they practiced not to eat pat akum, but in a place where they practiced to eat pat akum it is permitted even for eating; and this is what the Gemara says there — R' Yosi: bread was not, etc.; from which it implies they may permit it; and so there, 36, side 1: in our days you may permit, etc. — thus it did not spread in all places, as written there, 36, side 1, and R' Yosi the Nassi, etc., and Yerushalmi there, and first chapter of Shabbos, and chapter 2 of Pesachim; and the Rif brought here — bread: R' Yaakov in the name of R' Yochanan: bread of the customs of ignoramuses — where pat Yisrael is available, by right pat akum should be forbidden, and they leaned on it and permitted it because of saving life; and the sages of Caesarea in the name of R' Yaakov like the view of those who permit, but only from the palter; and even though in the Gemara there Rav Dimi and Rav Yosef thought, etc. — the conclusion there is not so, for R' Chelbo and R' Yochanan certainly did not argue in error; rather there are certainly Amoraim who disagree with Rav Dimi and Rav Yosef and hold it was permitted by the court, even according to the view that, etc., and like the Yerushalmi cited above; and the sages of Caesarea in the name of R' Yochanan like the view of those who permit. Ran. And he wrote: and that which R' Yosi said bread was not permitted by the court — meaning they did not seat a court on this, only incidentally he revealed his view; and the Rosh explained: it was not permitted by a court to permit in all places, only the decree did not spread in all places, as above. And to us it appears that above it says from which, etc.; and furthermore R' Yochanan said even according to the view that, etc. — from which for him it does not apply, only we do not rule like him, since the Gemara brought R' Chelbo's and R' Yochanan's words even according to the view that, etc., as above — hence he holds like those who permit, and as written in the Yerushalmi cited above; and in Avodat HaTaharah, chapter 8, section 1; and even though the Gemara says there Rava said, etc., do not speak, etc. — that is regarding that which is forbidden, such as baal habayit's, for Rava himself permits in Pesachim, 30, side 1; and see Rashi there, s.v. sons, etc.; and there, 41: the baraita teaches: a shop, etc., even for eating; and the anonymous teaching is bread — therefore the Mishnah did not teach "permitted for eating," as above; and see Tosafot, Avodah Zarah, 13, side 2, s.v. R' Yirmiyah, etc.`,
    '2#ב': `In a place, etc. Gemara there — R' Chelbo said, etc.; and he holds R' Chelbo and R' Yochanan disagree, like the second view the Ran wrote; and requires study why he ruled like R' Chelbo and not R' Yochanan — and this is like the Ran's version in the Rambam; but our version in the Rambam reads: in a place where there is no Jewish baker, and in the field because, etc.; and so in Kesef Mishneh, that a corrupted version came to the Ran; and he explains the first view in the Ran that R' Yochanan adds to R' Chelbo stringently and ruled like R' Yochanan; and he further wrote: some say R' Yochanan adds to R' Chelbo leniently — one of them suffices; and so in Torat HaBayit.`,
    '2#ג': `And some say, etc. As above, that it did not spread in all places, as written in the Yerushalmi cited above; and we who practice leniency do so from places where it did not spread, and therefore it is permitted. Mordechai. And from the Yerushalmi cited above it appears it is permitted even where pat Yisrael is available, as written: where pat Yisrael, etc., and they permitted it.`,
    '2#ד': `But bread, etc. Yerushalmi cited above; and only, etc.; and so in the Gemara they said palter. Torat HaBayit.`,
    '2#ה': `And it is not called, etc. As the Rashba wrote: the reason for palter leniency is that whatever he made to sell — in buying it there is no familiarity; and so they said in Challah, chapter 2: a baker who is, etc.; and likewise a woman, etc.`,
    '3#_': `Some say, etc. So too the Ra'ah; and when he dies, he holds we follow the present, for the reason is familiarity, and he disagrees with the Rashba who holds we follow the beginning, as written in seif 7; and the Rashba wrote explicitly that even if he ordered him it is permitted — and see below.`,
    '4#_': `Place, etc. The reduction is because of distress.`,
    '6#א': `In a place, etc. As written in second chapter of Yoreh De'ah, 16, side 2; and Avodah Zarah, 38, side 1 — it teaches us flour is primary; and see Tosafot there and there, and in Chullin, chapter 4, section 1.`,
    '6#ב': `But infanda, etc. For the oiliness is visible on it; and also if absorbed, from the beginning it was visible and was forbidden because of bishulei akum and forbids the dough — unlike dough kneaded with eggs where from the beginning it was nullified in it. See there and there.`,
    '6#ג': `And some forbid, etc. As above; and the first view holds that since it is only for appearance, it is not a matter of concern, as Rashi wrote in chapter 5 of Zevachim (see above, siman 102, s.k. 6).`,
    '6#ד': `And those kneaded doughs, etc. For the way of its baking he makes them; and furthermore, if one establishes a meal upon them he recites ha-Motzi. From what is written in chapter Kol Sha'ah (42, side 1). Tosafot, Beit Yosef there; but if it were not in the category of bread it would be forbidden because of bishulei akum, as written above, seif 1.`,
    '7#_': `Pat baal habayit, etc. Avodat HaTaharah; and he means that in his presence we are concerned lest he take, as written above, even though it is now in the gentile's hand.`,
    '8#_': `Some say, etc. It is not as stringent as demai, as Tosafot wrote, 35, side 2, s.v. from which; and we learned (third chapter of Demai): they feed, etc., and the guest of demai; and we say there in the Yerushalmi: even a Jewish guest — because of distress.`,
    '9#א': `And he stirred the fire slightly. Not as Tosafot wrote there, s.v. and he came — specifically one who brings his cooking close; and this is what it means "and even if not," etc., that the matter is not so important. Rambam; and see Tosafot there: (collection) And even if not, etc. — and the Ramban and R' Yitzchak disagree; and R' Yitzchak wrote that it appears in places where they practiced eating pat akum they instituted kisam for distancing; and in Avodat HaTaharah, chapter 9, section 1 (end).`,
    '9#ב': `And if he blew, etc. As written in Bava Kamma, 18, side 2: a rooster, etc.; and we say there, 17, side 2: R' said: granted, Sumchus, etc.; he retracted, etc.; and if so, here too it is like stoking by hand, for it is like his body.`,
    '10#א': `If they baked, etc. For the first heatings help the last heating more than kisam and stoking; and all the more so since channels remain from the first heatings.`,
    '10#ב': `And some say, etc. Likewise as above, that the first heat helps the later heatings in such a case.`,
    '11#א': `Bread, etc. From that in chapter Kol Sha'ah and there, 65, side 2.`,
    '11#ב': `And if he split, etc. As written in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah, 64, side 1: unless they are crushed, etc., for there is recognition; and the same applies here.`,
    '11#ג': `And therefore they practiced, etc. As written there; and similarly, etc.; therefore, etc.`,
    '12#_': `If a gentile baked, etc., and some say, etc. For leniency they said afterward like a court is nothing; but closeness in a Jew's cooking always helps, as written above, section 4: if, etc.; and for a Jew it helps even slight stoking, as above; and see siman 113, seif 40, in the gloss.`,
    '13#א': `One who is not, etc., he cuts, etc. As Tosafot wrote, Berachot, 39, side 2, s.v. but — from the Yerushalmi: impure bread is clean and cemetery bread is pure — he blesses on whichever he wishes.`,
    '13#ב': `And it is permitted, etc. As written (Yevamos, 7, side 2; Zevachim, 32, side 2): since it was permitted for his leprosy, etc.; and see Avodat HaTaharah.`,
    '14#א': `(Collection) Kutach, etc. For it is exempt from challah. Torat HaBayit in the name of the Rosh (end).`,
    '14#ב': `And there is no, etc., and likewise, etc. It is not as stringent as demai, for we say in Chullin, 6, side 1, that they did not decree, etc., even moist; and even though one may say the substance is from the Torah, all the more so here, and all the more so dry. Mordechai, first chapter of Chullin there.`,
    '14#ג': `But, etc. As written there: this case is different there, since he said, etc.`,
    '15#א': `One who is careful, etc. As above; and as written in the Yerushalmi (end of Challah): challah of, etc. — R' Avahu taught in its harvesting that it requires a majority; R' Yonah teaches it rises in less than a kav and is not forbidden. Another version: a small Mishnah said so, and it is eaten with a non-priest on the table — meaning in one bowl; and as written in siman 323; and even according to Tosafot who wrote we do not rule like the Yerushalmi — nevertheless we learn that what is permitted in mixture is permitted in such a case.`,
    '15#ב': `Some say, etc. As written in chapter 4 of Demai: one who imposes a vow on his fellow, etc.; and they said in the Yerushalmi there: R' Yanai in the house of R' Yishmael in the name of R' Yochanan: on the Sabbath of Protogema because of enmity; R' Avun said: here they permitted tevel because of ways of peace; and see Rambam there in commentary and in his composition that for R' Avin even without Protogema it is permitted.`,
    '15#ג': `That it is, etc., and there is no, etc. That is to say, regarding butter it is forbidden in such a case, for bread is different since it is the essence of the meal; and we say in fourth chapter of Pesachim that even to be lenient there is change because of dispute, etc., that it is not a d'oraisa prohibition, as the Rosh wrote there from that which we say there (second version, side 2): and there is no change of dispute because of, etc.; unlike butter — go out and see how many are in the market who do not eat butter, as written: go out, etc. (Rosh's version there).`,
    '16#_': `Some say, etc. As written in chapter 3 of Pesachim (46, side 1) and chapter 9 of Chullin: to knead, etc.; and according to Tosafot's explanation there in the name of the Arukh: a person who eats quickly and has a kneader before him, etc.; and likewise regarding pat akum, as Tosafot wrote in Berachot; and compare to tumah, as written in seif 13.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `1) [Seif 1] The Sages forbade eating pat akum because of marriage concern. So in the Gemara, Avodah Zarah, daf 35, side 2; and we say there in the Gemara, daf 36, side 2: they decreed on their bread and oil because of their wine, and on their wine because of their daughters, and on their daughters because of something else — meaning, if you permit him to eat with you at one gathering, afterward he will come to drink his wine and marry his daughter and worship idolatry. Shulchan Gavoah, section 1.`,
    '2#_': `2) There. The Sages forbade eating pat akum, etc. — it appears the view of the Mechaber and the Rav throughout this siman that there is no prohibition in pat akum except when a gentile baked it; and as Beit Yosef wrote according to the Rambam and Rashba that even though a gentile kneaded and shaped, whatever a Jew did in the baking is permitted; and so the Rav in Turei Chayim, general rule 71, law 2, and he brought there many later authorities who hold thus. Shach, s.k. 1.`,
    '3#_': `3) And even bread that the gentile made for himself is permitted through throwing kisam or chitui, like pat Yisrael that a gentile baked — which is permitted through throwing kisam or chitui — below in seif 9, for one should not distinguish between bread and bread, as the Rambam holds, and not like the Ran. Beit Yosef. Turei Chayim there. Bach. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Beit Yosef, section 5. Peri Chadash, end of section 1. Parshat Torah, section 2. Birkei Yosef on Shiyurei Berachah, section 18. Aruch HaShulchan, sections 20 and 25.`,
    '4#_': `4) There. The Sages forbade eating pat akum, etc. — and even a Samaritan who is not an idolater — his bread is forbidden. Parshat Torah, end of section 3, section 84, section 2, and he wrote it is plain.`,
    '5#_': `5) And regarding bread of Karaites one should be stringent and forbid their bread like gentiles, for they too do not eat our bread. Peri Chadash, section 2. Lechem HaPanim, section 2. Binyamin Zeev, section 1. Section 84 there. However, what they wrote that they do not eat from our bread is no proof, only because they regard them as gentiles and there is marriage concern among them. Shulchan Gavoah, section 2.`,
    '6#_': `6) And Samaritan bread — one should not be more stringent than other gentiles' bread. Peri Chadash there. And in a place where they permitted gentile bread, the same applies to Samaritans. Section 84 there. However, in Sefer Kaftor VaFerach, chapter 5, daf 17, it appears Samaritans persist in their prohibitions — see there, Birkei Yosef on Shiyurei Berachah, section 4; and therefore one should be stringent where possible.`,
    '7#_': `7) And it is permitted to buy gentile leaven and ferment dough with it, even for one who is careful about pat akum. So Beit Yosef to prove in this siman; and so Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Beit Yosef, section 3; and Yeshuot Yaakov agreed, end of section 1, Chacham Adam, general rule 65, section 20. But regarding crumbs that are visible, they are certainly forbidden as long as they were not nullified in the majority. Taz at the end of this siman. Lechem HaPanim, section 1. Binyamin Zeev, section 1. However, in places where worms are found in water or flour, it is forbidden to buy from gentiles even pat palter or leaven because of worms if they are not careful about cleanliness and do not strain the water and do not sift the flour. And see above, siman 84, sections 115 and 116. And regarding challah, if one bought leaven from a gentile, see below, siman 324, seif 11, regarding whether the leaven is exempt — see there.`,
    '8#_': `8) There. Marriage concern. But regarding gentile revulsion there is no concern, for ordinarily their vessels are not ben yomo, as below, siman 122, seif 6 — see there and in our words there.`,
    '9#_': `9) There, gloss. And even in a place where there is no marriage concern it is forbidden — such as bread of heretics who have no children; and likewise bread of youths, young men, and eunuchs — even though they have no sons and daughters it is forbidden. Rashba in his responsum there, siman 248. Taz, s.k. 1. Shach, s.k. 4. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Taz, section 2. Peri Chadash, section 4. Lechem HaPanim, section 4. Binyamin Zeev, section 2. Erech HaShulchan, section 2. Chacham Adam, general rule 65, section 1.`,
    '10#_': `10) And likewise bread made in the royal house and in ministers' houses is forbidden, even though certainly the king does not intermarry with us — for the Sages did not differentiate. Knesset HaGedolah there. Binyamin Zeev there. Erech HaShulchan there. Mishbetzot Zahav, section 1.`,
    '11#_': `11) And the same applies to an apostate to idolatry — there is no marriage prohibition in his daughters, since he is like a full gentile and is in their decree, and his bread is forbidden. Erech HaShulchan there. And what Parshat Torah, section 1, wrote to permit appears because he did not see Erech HaShulchan's words cited above, who brought proof from the Ran — see there; and see below, siman 113, section 1.`,
    '12#_': `12) Gentile bread through cooking is forbidden — not like one who wished to permit it: where did its prohibition go? Maharil in his responsum, siman 195. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Taz, section 8. Hilchot Ketanot, volume 2, siman 83. Lechem HaPanim, section 9. Binyamin Zeev, section 12. Minchat Yonatan in Solut leMinchah at the end of Turei Chayim, general rule 71, law 1. Birkei Yosef on Shiyurei Berachah, section 20. And in his book Yosef Ometz, siman 79. Zivchei Tzedek, section 7. Ben Avraham, parashat Chukat, section 4.`,
    '13#_': `13) There. And they only forbade bread of the five species of grain, etc. — for this is an important matter and leads to familiarity. Taz, s.k. 2.`,
    '14#_': `14) And if one mixed other species with the five species of grain, we follow the majority — even with rice, regarding pat akum one may be lenient. Mishbetzot Zahav, section 2.`,
    '15#_': `15) If one found bread on the road, we follow the majority of passersby. Eruvin, 64, side 2. And if they are half and half, it is a d'rabbanan doubt and we rule leniently. Erech HaShulchan, section 4; and he wrote, and not like Peri Chadash, section 13, who was stringent. Section 84, section 13. Sdei Chemed, section 2. Chiddushei Ra'ah Kook.`,
    '16#_': `16) There, gloss. And also it is not forbidden because of bishulei akum if it does not go upon a king's table. It appears he understood the condition he set — "and if it does not go upon a king's table" — applies to bread too; but this is not so, as proven from the Tur's words that regarding bread there is no doubt that plainly no rice or millet bread goes upon a king's table, but in a cooked dish it is possible it goes upon a king's table. Bach. And so Rabbeinu Yerucham, Netiv 17, chapter 7, that legume bread, etc., has no concern of bishulei akum nor of pat akum since it does not go upon a king's table; and Beit Yosef brought it in Bedek HaBayit and wrote that such is the Tur's view. And so the Levush. Taz, s.k. 3. Shach, s.k. 5. Knesset HaGedolah in Hagahot Beit Yosef, section 8. Binyamin Zeev, section 3. Minchat Yonatan on Turei Chayim, general rule 71, section 18. Mishbetzot Zahav, section 3. Chacham Adam, general rule 65, section 1 — and he means that presumably we say it does not go upon a king's table and one need not be concerned, and it is permitted to eat baal habayit's bread.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 112, Shulchan Aruch, seif 1) The Sages forbade eating pat of nations. See Shach, s.k. 1, who brought in the name of the Ran that whatever a gentile kneaded and shaped, and even if a Jew baked, is also called pat akum; and one may challenge this view from that which we say in Pesachim: with pastries of a gentile a person may fill his stomach from them provided he eats an olive-size of matzah at the end — thus pat akum is permitted when a Jew baked. But to my mind it is already explained in the Ran himself there and in Beit Yosef at the beginning of this siman that specifically where the flour belongs to the gentile is there concern of familiarity; whereas when the flour belongs to a Jew and only the gentile kneaded and shaped, the dough is not forbidden because of pat akum; and "pastries of gentiles" deals with where the dough belongs to a Jew.`,
    '2#_': `(There) Pat akum. It appears that if he is not an idolater the bread was not forbidden; and this is because he explains according to the commentators that that which it says "on their bread," etc., and "on their wine because of their daughters," and "on their daughters because of something else" — according to the conclusion that "on their daughters" refers to seclusion with their daughters, it also applies to that which says "on their wine because of their daughters," that "because of their daughters" in the first clause also means seclusion with their daughters, and they did not decree seclusion with their daughters except because of "something else"; and if so, where there is no concern of "something else" there is no decree at all on their bread and wine. But there are those who explain that that which the conclusion says "because of their daughters" means seclusion, and it applies only to that which says "on their daughters because of something else"; but in the first clause, "on their wine because of their daughters" means the prohibition of "you shall not intermarry with them," and that which it says "on their daughters because of something else" is a matter in its own right; accordingly the decree on their bread does not depend on idolatry — even one who is not an idolater, his bread is forbidden because of "you shall not intermarry," etc.`,
    '3#_': `(There, in the gloss) And also it is not forbidden because of bishulei akum. See Shach, s.k. 5, in the name of the Bach, that he did not discuss legume bread at all but a legume cooked dish; one may infer that if so, what does it come to teach us — this matter is obvious, that a gentile's cooking is nothing unless it certainly goes upon a king's table; and one must say that it teaches us that regarding other cooked dishes, if we do not know with certainty that it does not go upon a king's table, it is forbidden even if one knows with certainty that its type goes upon a king's table; whereas regarding legume bread, even if one does not know with certainty that it does not go upon a king's table, it is permitted, for presumably it does not go upon a king's table. See Taz, s.k. 3; and furthermore one may say not like the Bach — that which we do not distinguish regarding pat akum between whether it goes upon a king's table or not is because ordinarily bread is called as going upon a king's table, since the essence of a meal is bread and "by bread shall man live"; whereas legume bread is not so important and is called as not going upon a king's table; but certainly in a place where even legume bread goes upon a king's table, etc., it is also forbidden, for even regarding bread one may distinguish between going and not going; and now one may say that the Rama also discusses legume bread, and there is no place for the Bach's challenge; investigate.`,
    '4#_': `(There, seif 2, in the gloss) And some say that even in a place where pat Yisrael is available, etc. See Shach, s.k. 18; and for the Rama one must say certainly that for one who permits palter bread, in a place where pat Yisrael is available, pat baal habayit is likewise permitted because the prohibition did not spread; and that which he did not bring the second view even regarding pat baal habayit is because he does not wish to rely on the Mordechai's reasoning alone unless combined with palter bread, since most poskim hold that pat palter was not forbidden at all, only pat baal habayit; and even though we do not rule thus, nevertheless combined with the Mordechai one may rely on oneself; investigate.`,
    '5#_': `(There, seif 3) Some say that if a palter, etc. See Shach, s.k. 12, what he challenged from below, seif 7, that we follow the beginning — to my mind, the poskim challenge regarding palter bread: why is it permitted? We should be concerned lest he baked for himself, and if so it is pat baal habayit. And one may answer: since we see he sold this bread, presumably he baked it for that purpose from the outset. If so, this applies only when we see he sells it; but if a Jew ordered him in his home, we are certainly concerned lest he baked for his own need, and in such a case even the Rashba agrees it is forbidden; and so appears somewhat in Nekudat HaKesef to the Rav Shach — see there.`,
    '6#_': `(There, seif 6, in the gloss) And some forbid bread with eggs smeared, etc. This law the Rama wrote in the name of Tosafot, and in Tosafot Beitzah, daf 16, side 1, s.v. it teaches us that flour is primary — it appears even if they smeared the eggs on its surface there is no concern of gentile cooking; and so the Ran there explicitly in the name of Tosafot that even if he smeared on its surface it is permitted. However, the Rama, of blessed memory, in Darkei Moshe infers thus from the words of Tosafot in Chullin, daf 64, and the Mordechai there, that it appears there that if the eggs are visible on the bread it is forbidden — see there.`,
  },
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `The laws of pat akum. It contains 16 seifim: The Sages forbade eating pat of gentiles, idolaters, because of marriage concern {Rama: (and even in a place where there is no marriage concern it is forbidden) (Rashba, siman 248)} and they only forbade pat of the five species of grain; but pat of legumes, rice, and millet is not in the category of ordinary pat that they forbade: {Rama: Also it is not forbidden because of bishulei akum if it does not go upon a king's table (Tur and Beit Yosef in the name of the Rosh's responsum):}`,
    '2#main': `There are places that are lenient in the matter and buy bread from the baker, the non-Jew, in a place where there is no Jewish baker because it is a time of distress {Rama: (and some say that even in a place where pat Yisrael is available it is permitted) (Beit Yosef according to the Mordechai, Semak, Hagahot Maimoniyot, Maharai, and Issur VeHeter, beginning of general rule 44)} but pat baal habayit — there is no one who rules leniently regarding it, for the essence of the decree is because of marriage concern, and if one eats pat baal habayit he will come to dine with them: {Rama: Pat baal habayit is only so called if he made it for the members of his household; but if he made it to sell it is called palter even if that is not his usual practice; and likewise a palter who made it for himself is called baal habayit (so it appears in Beit Yosef):}`,
    '3#main': `Some say that if a palter ordered a Jew, his bread is like pat baal habayit.`,
    '4#main': `In a place where a Jewish palter is not available — according to those who permit — one may buy bread from a gentile palter; if a Jewish palter arrived there, the bread of the gentile palter is forbidden until the Jewish palter sells his bread; and after the Jew's bread is finished, the gentile's bread returns to being permitted.`,
    '5#main': `Some say that one who has bread in hand, or there is a Jewish palter and a gentile palter, who makes bread finer than his or of another type that the Jewish palter does not have — may buy from the gentile palter in a place where they practiced leniency regarding pat palter, for since his mind is more at ease with that palter's bread because of its importance in his eyes, this is bread of distress for him.`,
    '6#main': `In a place where they practiced leniency regarding pat palter, even if it is kneaded with eggs or eggs are smeared on its surface it is permitted; but infanda that a non-Jew baked is forbidden to eat from its bread (see above, siman 113, seif 3): {Rama: And some forbid bread with eggs smeared on its surface because they are visible and are not nullified relative to the bread and there is concern of bishulei akum (Tosafot, Semag, Hagahot Ashiri, Arukh, Mordechai, chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah, and Semak, siman 207) and so is the practice) And those kneaded doughs called kichlach or kinds of sweets called lekuch are in the category of bread; and in a place where they practiced leniency regarding pat akum they too are permitted, and we do not say there is concern of bishulei akum. And there are kinds of kneaded dough called kichlach that are baked on irons and the iron is greased at the time of baking with milk or pork — regarding those one should be careful and forbid them, and so is the custom (Maharai in Hagahot ShaDa and Agur in the name of R' Yosef of Prizish):}`,
    '7#main': `Pat baal habayit is forbidden forever, even if a palter bought it from him, and even if he sent it to a Jew in his home; and likewise even if a Jew sent it to another it is forbidden forever. And pat palter is permitted forever, even if baal habayit bought it from him — for this prohibition does not follow whoever has the bread in hand now, but rather whoever had it at the time of baking.`,
    '8#main': `Some say that in a place where no palter is available at all, even baalei batim's bread is permitted {Rama: (and one need not wait for kosher bread, and so is the practice) (Beit Yosef in the name of Orach Chayim):}`,
    '9#main': `If a non-Jew lit the oven and a Jew baked in it, or a Jew lit and a non-Jew baked, or the non-Jew lit and the non-Jew baked and a Jew came and stirred the fire slightly — behold, it is permitted; and even if he only threw one piece of wood into the oven he permits all the bread in it, for the matter is only to be a sign that their bread is forbidden. {Rama: (And if he blew on the fire it is like chitui) (also siman 113, and in Agur in the name of R' Yosef Molcho, and it is in the responsum of Maharil, 221):}`,
    '10#main': `If they baked in the oven through a non-Jew three times in one day and kashered the oven through kisam on the first two times and on the third time did not kasher it — it is permitted {Rama: (Mordechai)}: {Rama: And some say that if the oven was kashered once and the oven did not stand from time to time without heating, even if they baked in it thus many days, everything is permitted by force of the first kashering (Arukh, general rule 74, and Agur) and one may rely on this:}`,
    '11#main': `Pat Yisrael that a non-Jew baked without Jewish chitui and without kisam is forbidden {Rama: (and forbidden)} to sell to a gentile lest he come to sell to a Jew; and if he split the loaf in two it is permitted to sell to a gentile: {Rama: And the same applies to all forbidden pat akum; and therefore the practice is not to buy pieces of bread from the gentile, for we are concerned lest it is forbidden bread that a Jew sold to him thus (Mordechai, chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah, and Arukh):}`,
    '12#main': `If a non-Jew baked the bread without Jewish chitui and without kisam, even if the surface of the bread crusted in the oven, Jewish chitui is effective as long as the bread still needs the oven and still improves in its baking; and some say that even if he removed the bread there is a remedy to return it to the oven through a Jew if it still improves.`,
    '13#main': `One who is not careful about pat akum who reclined with a baal habayit who is careful about pat akum, and on the table is pat Yisrael and pat akum finer than the Jew's — the baal habayit cuts from the finer, and he is permitted for that entire meal with pat akum.`,
    '14#main': `Kutach of a gentile is permitted, and we are not concerned about pat akum in it: {Rama: And likewise wherever pat akum became mixed with other food it is nullified in the majority, whether moist or dry; but it is forbidden to mix in order to eat it (Arukh):}`,
    '15#main': `One who is careful about pat akum may eat in a bowl with one who is not careful about it, and even though the taste of pat akum mixes into pat Yisrael he is not concerned: {Rama: Some say that one who is careful about pat akum and eats with others who are not careful may eat with them because of enmity and quarrel, since if he does not eat with them bread, which is the essence of the meal, they permitted him because of enmity; and one may not learn from here to other prohibitions (Beit Yosef in the name of an Ashkenazic responsum, and it is the responsum of Maharil, siman 35):}`,
    '16#main': `Some say that one who is careful about pat akum and is on the road — if there is pat Yisrael within four mil he should wait: {Rama: (and it was already explained above that the practice is to be lenient):}`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 112, in Shach, s.k. 12, gloss) And accordingly it is difficult, for the Mechaber, etc. And to me it appears that it is different here, since the palter ordered the Jew — it is an obvious matter that he made it for himself, as the Rav wrote above from his own words nearby — and therefore the Mechaber did not write the Rav's law because it is all the more so; investigate.`,
    '2#_': `(There, in Shach, s.k. 13) To buy, etc. — requires study, etc. Incidentally he also wrote that the Gaon of blessed memory understood "lekach" with a kamatz, and the explanation is: if one bought bread from a gentile palter, etc.; but this is only "likach" with a chirik, and it refers to the view of those who permit buying; investigate.`,
    '3#_': `(There, in Taz, s.k. 9) And to me, the younger, it seems astonishing, etc. There is no wonder at them, for as their words are explained explicitly in the responsum of Maharam of Rothenburg, siman 154, and these are his words: we recite in the Beit Yosef that kutach of a gentile is permitted — if because of bread that is in it, there is no reason to forbid it in itself; but it is astonishing, for it is forbidden because of milk that a gentile milked and no Jew sees it. And one may say that perhaps impure milk does not stand, and therefore kutach is not visible — end of his words. And if so, perforce one must say that kutach was different in the time of the Gemara from this kutach; or, as the Bach wrote, and these are his words: and to us, kutach and butter are both not in the category of the decree on milk and cheese; and regarding butter they practiced prohibition in some places because milk remained in the crevices; but in kutach, where there are no crevices, they did not practice prohibition anywhere — and this too is within Rashal's words — end of his words. And if so, to my mind, Babylonian kutach is certainly different, for it was not thick and there is much admixture in it; and that which we say in curdling kemakha — and so R' Chananel wrote that curdling for kutach is forbidden — that is when a gentile curdles for himself; forbidden in curdling for kutach since it is milk before us and is in the category of the decree on milk that a gentile milked; and furthermore it is possible he chooses kutach that is not thick, but kutach that gentiles sell to others — since it is not milk before us and it stands — is permitted. In Beit Yosef, daf 102, side 4; and even though before this he answered two Talmuds, etc., I did not understand his intent in this, for according to what he answered, it is like one who mixes with his hands — all the more so one should not say it is permitted here with gentile leaven because they did not decree on mixtures of pat akum since he mixes the leaven with his hands; and so too in Hagahot ShaDa, siman 75, that l'chatchila one should not put gentile bread in a bowl — plainly forbidden, for it is like mixing with hands — end of his words; and requires study.`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `Marriage concern. Avodat HaTaharah; and see in Sefer Tiferet Moshe, siman 113, seif 9, who wrote that bread of an apostate is permitted, for there is no marriage concern, for even though he sinned he is a Jew and one may marry his daughter — see there.`,
    '2#_': `Baker. See in the responsa of Mayim Rabim, from siman 5 through siman 15, and the content of his words there to forbid the bread from that baker, since the baker also deals with fat of another substance and it is impossible that it not become mixed in the bread, whether at the time of kneading, shaping, or baking, and perhaps it became so mixed that there is imparting of taste; therefore even when the baker says he is very careful, it is forbidden — see there; and see also in the responsum of Devar Yosef, siman 28, and responsum of Shemesh Tzedakah, Yoreh De'ah, siman 47, on this matter.`,
    '3#_': `And forbidden to sell, etc. The Mordechai learned this law in chapter 2 of Avodah Zarah from that in chapter Kol Sha'ah regarding the boat that sank in a swamp; and it is astonishing how he learned from there to here. One may say it is different there, for it is a Torah prohibition, whereas here it is a rabbinic prohibition of sheluhot — perhaps for d'rabbanan we are not concerned for this doubt that perhaps he will sell them to a Jew; and it would have been better for the Mordechai to bring proof for this from Avodah Zarah, daf 65, regarding wheat upon which yayin nesech fell, according to what the Mordechai himself wrote in chapter Kol Sha'ah, section 525, that that incident deals with a rabbinic doubt — see there; and see in the responsum of Noda B'Yehuda, second series, Orach Chayim part, siman 70, and Yoreh De'ah part, section 286; and see in Shach above, siman 57, s.k. 51.`,
    '4#_': `Who is not careful. See Taz and Shach; and see in the responsum of Geonei Batra'i, siman 18; and see in the responsum of Bach, the new responsa, siman 27.`,
    '5#_': `Nullified in the majority. [And see in the responsum of Chavot Yair, siman 100, what he wrote on this; see Peri Megadim, Orach Chayim, in the conduct of deceiving gentiles, third order, section 37, who wrote that it is reasonable that pat akum, bishulei akum, and their butter that became mixed in less than a majority and was forbidden, and afterward fell into another dish — we do not say chein nafsho (to require a majority against everything that was forbidden initially), even though regarding other d'rabbanan prohibitions we say chein nafsho, and even a clinging prohibition as proven in siman 72 regarding heart and elsewhere, because of the taste in Beit Yosef among those who were lenient that sixty is not required — it is not interchanged with meat and milk — see there.]`,
    '6#_': `Four mil he should wait. In Sefer Chachmat Adam, general rule 65, he wrote that in any event, according to this view, the measure of a mil — one must walk even if he is not going there, for we establish regarding a kneader and prayer following him up to a mil; and see in the responsum of Beit Yosef, siman 35, that there is no distinction between riding on a horse or walking on foot, and likewise if the road is bad; but in this there is a distinction whether he walks alone or walks with his children and household — see there.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 112, seif 1) To eat bread. If one found bread on the road we follow the majority of passersby (Eruvin, 64, side 2). And it appears that if they are half and half it is a d'rabbanan doubt and we rule leniently; see Maharsha there.`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["Bread of nations, etc. It appears the view of the Mechaber and the Rav throughout this siman that there is no prohibition in pat akum except when a gentile baked it; and as Beit Yosef wrote according to the Rambam and Rashba that even though a gentile kneaded and shaped, whatever a Jew did in the baking is permitted; and therefore the Mechaber did not write the distinctions of kneading and shaping that Beit Yosef wrote, because all that Beit Yosef wrote is specifically according to the Ran and Tur, whereas not according to the Rambam and Rashba; investigate; and so the Rav in Turei Chayim, general rule 71, law 2, and he brought there many later authorities who hold thus:"]`,
    '1#ב': `["Idolaters. See above, siman 123, and siman 124, s.k. 12:"]`,
    '1#ג': `["Because of marriage concern. But because of gentile revulsion there is no concern, for ordinarily their vessels are not ben yomo, as below, siman 122:"]`,
    '1#ד': `["And even in a place where there is no marriage concern. Such as bread of heretics who have no children is forbidden — not because of the seller's or giver's daughter did they say, but because of gentiles' daughters: if this one has none, that one has. Rashba there:"]`,
    '1#ה': `["And also it is not forbidden, etc. The Bach challenged this and wrote that there is no doubt legume, rice, and millet bread does not go upon a king's table, as appears from the Tur's words; rather the Rosh deals with a cooked dish made from rice and millet, where there is room to distinguish whether it goes upon a king's table or not — end of his words; and so R' Yerucham, Netiv 17, chapter 7, that legume bread, etc., has no concern of bishulei akum nor pat akum since it does not go upon a king's table — so the commentators — end of his words; and Beit Yosef brings it in Bedek HaBayit and writes there that such is the Tur's view; and see Turei Chayim there, law 11, in the name of Issur VeHeter:"]`,
    '2#א': `["There are places, etc. And I would apply this to sheluhot in siman 113, that nowhere practiced leniency, for 'by bread shall man live' applies to it and the Yerushalmi reason of saving life applies; and why were they lenient also regarding bread through throwing kisam, and likewise several matters that do not help for sheluhot, as the Mechaber wrote in siman 113, seif 6:"]`,
    '2#ב': `["The gentile. Specifically pat akum; but pat Yisrael that a gentile baked — the Tur wrote that nowhere practiced permitting it, for it is like bishulei akum; and so Issur VeHeter, general rule 44, law 9, in the name of the Geonim' responsum, and it is from the Mordechai, chapter Ein Ma'amidin, who brought thus in the name of Maharam from the Geonim (and it is in Maharam's responsum, Prague edition, siman 33); and so Turei Chayim there, beginning of law 2; and it is explained in their words there that even though pat akum that a gentile baked — they practiced leniency regarding it; and so the Magen Avraham; and the reason they did not forbid such pat because of bishulei akum is that it is a decree the majority cannot abide by; therefore pat Yisrael that a gentile baked — that reason does not apply — is forbidden:"]`,
    '2#ג': `["And some say, etc. In the Mordechai he wrote the reason: because those places practicing leniency did so since the prohibition of pat akum did not spread at the beginning of the decree in those places, for pat akum's prohibition did not spread throughout Israel. Accordingly it appears even pat baalei batim is permitted, for the prohibition of pat akum did not spread at all in those places; and so is further proven in the Mordechai there — see there; and even though in the Yerushalmi, chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah, pat baalei batim is forbidden, and so hold some poskim — behold they too forbid gentile palter bread when a Jewish palter exists, because they hold the prohibition spread in all places, only they returned and permitted gentile palter bread where there is no Jewish palter because of saving life; but one who holds the prohibition did not spread in those places at all in any regard — is permitted. In this I settled there the correctness of what the Bach challenged against the Tur's words; and according to the second view, of blessed memory, there is no distinction, etc.; and again I found support for myself in Maggid Meisharim — see there; but from Issur VeHeter, beginning of general rule 44, the Rama, and Magen Avraham, it is clear that even those who practiced leniency regarding pat palter forbid pat baalei batim even when a Jewish palter exists:"]`,
    '2#ד': `["That even, etc. And the later authorities and the Rav in Turei Chayim, beginning of general rule 71, and the Bach wrote that such spread as custom; however Turei Chayim wrote there in the name of the poskim that on the Ten Days of Repentance one should be careful regarding pat akum; and so explained Orach Chaim, siman 242, seif 2 — see there; however it appears one should be careful all year not to buy pat akum from the palter where there is a Jewish palter, unless gentile bread is finer — then it is permitted, as below, seif 5:"]`,
    '2#ה': `["But pat baal habayit. This is the Rambam's view, but we do not rule thus, as below, seif 8:"]`,
    '2#ו': `["And likewise palter. Even if established and expert for the many — whatever he made for himself, there is concern of familiarity, for regarding this matter he is a baal habayit:"]`,
    '3#_': `That if a palter, etc. So too Orach Chayim in the name of the Ra'ah; and in the Ra'ah's words in Bedek HaBayit, daf 88, side 2, it is explained that this is according to his view that we do not follow the beginning but the present; but according to the poskim who hold palter bread is permitted forever, even if baal habayit bought it from a gentile, because we follow the beginning — it is permitted, for since at the time of baking it was permitted it is not forbidden again; and so the Rashba in Torat HaBayit wrote explicitly that even if the palter was ordered by him it is permitted since it was permitted at the time of baking; and accordingly it is difficult, for the Mechaber holds below, seif 7, that we follow the beginning — how did he write this law here? Perhaps for that reason they wrote in the name of "some say," or it is possible he holds according to his reasoning to distinguish that when ordered by him it is worse.`,
    '4#א': `["To buy, etc. Requires study, for Orach Chayim in the name of the Ra'ah that Beit Yosef brought did not write except that if a Jewish palter arrived there, the gentile palter's bread is forbidden as long as it is in the palter's hand; but if the Jew already bought it permissibly he did not write it is forbidden — and it appears it is permitted; and so appears from the Ra'ah's words in Bedek HaBayit there:"]`,
    '4#ב': `["Forbidden. That is according to the Mechaber, seif 2; but according to the second view in the gloss it is plainly permitted; investigate:"]`,
    '6#א': `["Even if it is kneaded with eggs. Why should we be concerned? If because of blood — the majority of eggs have no blood, as above, siman 66, seif 8. If because of eggs of an impure bird — they are not common among us, as above, siman 86, seif 2. If because of bishulei akum — eggs are forbidden; below, siman 113, seif 4, flour is primary — so the poskim; and see above, siman 86, and what he wrote there:"]`,
    '6#ב': `["But infanda, etc. See above, siman 113, s.k. 6, from this:"]`,
    '6#ג': `["And there is concern of bishulei akum. And it is comparable to sheluhot, for nowhere practiced leniency, as below in siman 113:"]`,
    '6#ד': `["Called kichlach. Specifically those whose batter is thick and have the status of bread — so Turei Chayim there, law 12 — see there:"]`,
    '9#_': `And if he blew, etc. See above, siman 113, s.k. 8, 9.`,
    '10#_': `And some say, etc. Hagahot ShaDa wrote that if one is in doubt whether a Jew kashered the oven or not, it is permitted, for it is a d'rabbanan doubt and we rule leniently; and so the Rav in Turei Chayim there, law 6, and other later authorities.`,
    '13#_': `One who is not careful, etc. And so the Mechaber in Orach Chaim, siman 168, seif 5; and so the Rav in Turei Chayim there, law 5; and the Mechaber wrote there at the beginning that one careful about pat akum who has clean pat akum and unclean pat Yisrael before him removes pat akum from the table until after ha-Motzi; the reason at the beginning is that no other Jew reclines with him; but at the end and here, since another Jew reclines with him, if he does not bless on clean bread there is enmity, as below, seif 15 in the gloss; and even though it appears there that there is enmity concern only when one careful eats with others who are not careful, but one who eats only with one who is not careful — there is no enmity, for what comparison is there between this and that? — nevertheless, since regarding ha-Motzi one must bless on the finest, if he does not bless there is great enmity; alternatively, specifically when both their breads are equal is there no enmity when there is only one who is not careful; but when the bread of one not careful is cleaner and finer and nevertheless he does not eat it, there is certainly enmity — so it appears to me; and with this it is reconciled that the Bach's challenge on Terumat HaDeshen from the Mordechai, chapter Keitzad Mevarchin, regarding what he brought in the name of the minister of Kutzi and challenged Torat HaBayit and the Mechaber on this law they wrote here, and explained the Mordechai's words in the name of Ravyah in chapter 1 of Avodah Zarah in a strange, far-fetched way — all his words are not plausible, as I challenged him at length in my books — see there; and I proved with many proofs that the explanation of the Mordechai's words is as Torat HaBayit, the Rav, and other later authorities understood; and so is explained explicitly in Agudah — see there.`,
    '14#א': `["Kutach, etc. And one need not be concerned about gentile milk, as the Perishah and Bach wrote in the name of Maharshal — see there; and so in the responsum of Maharam of Rothenburg, Prague edition, siman 154; and see above, siman 115:"]`,
    '14#ב': `["Whether dry. Even if it is an important item — so Darkei Moshe and Turei Chayim there, law 8, in the name of Issur VeHeter; and Issur VeHeter, general rule 43, law 6, wrote these laws apply even to actual bishulei akum; and so Turei Chayim there; and see above, siman 115, s.k. 17:"]`,
    '14#ג': `["But forbidden to mix, etc. Darkei Moshe wrote on this, and these are his words: and it appears to me that nevertheless one may be permitted to eat in one bowl and there is no reason to forbid because of taste that became mixed; they only forbade mixing l'chatchila to eat the bread itself; but regarding taste there is no concern; however Hagahot ShaDa forbids l'chatchila — end of his words; and I examined Hagahot ShaDa and did not find necessity to say it deals when one eats the bread itself; and in any case the bowl is a second vessel and he is not cooking, as written in siman 105, s.k. 4:"]`,
    '15#א': `["Because of enmity. See above, s.k. 21:"]`,
    '15#ב': `["And one may not learn from here to other prohibitions. Even gentile butter and the like — in a place where they practice prohibition, a light prohibition is still a prohibition; and as below, siman 115, that specifically regarding bread there is enmity because 'by bread shall man live'; but not regarding other foods, for many people have no desire to eat butter, and the like — there:"]`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `And even in a place where there is no marriage concern. Such as one who has no children — for the Sages did not differentiate.`,
    '1#ב': `Only bread of the five, etc. For this is an important matter and leads to familiarity.`,
    '1#ג': `If it does not go upon a king's table. The language of the Tur, who wrote "and also it is not important," etc., implies that presumably one need not ask about this whether it goes upon a king's table, but certainly it does not go; and so too in the Levush and Maharach, of blessed memory, regarding legume bread, etc.`,
    '2#_': `There are places that are lenient, etc. Because the decree on bread did not spread in all places at the time they decreed on it in the days of the Tannaim.`,
    '6#א': `Even if it is kneaded with eggs, etc. The reason: since the bread is permitted, the eggs are secondary to it; and infanda will be explained in siman 113, seif 3.`,
    '6#ב': `And we do not say there is concern in them, etc. Since if one establishes a meal upon them he recites ha-Motzi — therefore their law is like bread proper.`,
    '11#_': `Pat Yisrael, etc. In this it has the law of sheluhot mentioned in siman 113 — so wrote the Tur.`,
    '13#_': `One who is not careful, etc. In Mordechai, chapter Ein Ma'amidin, he wrote in the name of Ravyah, and these are his words: a Jew who eats pat akum and has clean bread in hand, and the baal habayit is careful about pat akum — since this mitzvah is incumbent upon him to cut, he cuts from the finer; proof from the Yerushalmi: clean bread is impure and cemetery bread is pure — on whichever he wishes he cuts; and since he was permitted to cut, he is permitted for the entire meal — end of his words. He wrote on this in Torat HaBayit, siman 32: Ravyah did not specifically mean only if an Israelite eating pat akum was reclining at the table, for without him too it is permitted; rather he expressed it thus because who would bring pat akum to the table when the baal habayit is careful about it — end of his words. At first glance the main explanation seems to be that since he brings proof from the Yerushalmi that rules regarding clean bread, etc., that the choice is in his hand — automatically in Ravyah's law too the reason is that since pat akum is available before the baal habayit, out of honor for the mitzvah he is permitted to cut from it. And even though we find in Mordechai, chapter Keitzad Mevarchin, in the name of the minister of Kutzi, that one careful about gentile bread should remove the clean gentile bread from the table and cut from Israel's bread — nevertheless we find in Agudah, chapter Keitzad Mevarchin, that there is a dispute in this, and some say even one who is careful blesses on whichever he wishes; and if so, Ravyah may hold like this view. In Issur VeHeter, section 44, he brought Ravyah's words in this language: one careful about pat akum who sits at a table with one who is not careful and has cleaner pat akum — since he is permitted to cut from it, he is permitted for the entire meal, for there is no honor of Heaven that he should cut from it and then separate — end of his words. It appears that what he wrote regarding one who must be with him, etc., and he wrote there in the margin on this that his explanation is that the cutter is not careful about pat akum, and what he wrote that he is permitted to cut means only to fulfill the cutting; but if the cutter is careful and the diners are not careful, it is certainly better for him to remove the clean bread from the table until after cutting — end of his words. And similarly Maharach explained that the Mordechai deals with a baal habayit who has one person in his house as a hired worker and the baal habayit eats with that lodger, and the mitzvah of cutting is upon the host and the baal habayit follows him — and therefore he is permitted in pat akum with him. The main novelty: the baal habayit, who is primary, becomes secondary to the host; and he wrote: if the baal habayit makes the meal, he certainly must cut from Israel's bread since he does not wish to eat pat akum, and he need not even remove it from the table; and he disagreed with Shulchan Aruch on this. Had they seen Ravyah's language as copied in Agudah they would not have written thus, for it states there, chapter Ein Ma'amidin, in this language in the name of Ravyah: one careful about pat akum who has at his table an Israelite who is not careful and has fine bread on the table; and we say in the Yerushalmi: clean bread, etc.; and since he is permitted to cut from it, he is permitted to eat it for the entire meal, etc. — end of his words. Behold before you that the one careful is the host and he is primary, only that he has a guest who is not careful, as the language "sits with him" teaches that the one sitting is secondary at his table; and if so, the baal habayit must cut — therefore he blesses on the finer. But the main explanation is like Maharai's explanation: regarding the permission of the cutting itself he brings the Yerushalmi, and he holds it applies even to one who is careful; nevertheless there is a difficulty on Shulchan Aruch, for according to this ruling even one careful may cut on pat akum, not like the minister of Kutzi who commanded to remove; and above in Orach Chaim, siman 168, he ruled like the minister of Kutzi that one removes what is not Israel's — end of his words. It appears he explains Ravyah's words because the guest who is not careful will certainly eat pat akum since it is clean, and the law requires cutting from the bread one will eat, as Maharai wrote in Torat HaBayit, siman 32, that one should not cut first except from what one will eat; and if so, the baal habayit who must cut and cuts and brings out the guest cuts from what the guest will eat; and since he was permitted to cut for his sake, he was permitted for the entire meal for his sake, even though afterward each may eat his own bread — nevertheless, since at the time of cutting they need one bread, it was also permitted afterward; and so that the guest himself should not be obligated to cut on pat Yisrael, for this he brings from the Yerushalmi that one not careful cuts on whatever he wishes; and the same applies to this guest for whom the baal habayit cuts on his behalf. And so is the main halachah according to the ruling of Shulchan Aruch here and in Orach Chaim, siman 168 — but not for one careful who eats alone, as arose in Maharai's view, for in his explanation there is strain in saying Ravyah did not specifically mean an Israelite eating with him who is not careful; so it appears to my clear mind.`,
    '14#_': `And we are not concerned about bread, etc. And one should not forbid because of gentile milk, for impure milk does not stand; and if we say there is a slight admixture of milk in it, nevertheless it is nullified in its minority since it is mixed in, and it is not comparable to butter in siman 115 which is not mixed in — end of Rashal's words; and in Derishah and Maharach, of blessed memory, they brought it; and to me, the younger, it seems astonishing how he said milk admixture is nullified in its minority, for we say at the beginning of chapter Elu Overin: Babylonian kutach dulls the heart because of milk admixture in it — and if it were nullified it would not dull; and furthermore it is stated explicitly in chapter Ein Ma'amidin (Avodah Zarah, daf 35) that one who curdled kemakha — which is kutach — the milk is forbidden; and so in Mordechai it is written explicitly in chapter Ein Ma'amidin in the name of Rabbenu Tam that milk that a gentile milked — for eating and for kutach I forbid — end of his words; and so in Issur VeHeter, general rule 44, law 5. And it appears to my mind that kutach of a gentile is certainly forbidden; rather, here it deals with when it was made with supervision that a Jew sees it, only that the non-Jew puts his bread there, for there is no prohibition of pat akum in that — so it appears to my clear mind.`,
    '16#_': `Above. At the beginning of this siman the Beit Yosef proved that one may buy gentile leaven and ferment dough with it, even for one who is careful about pat akum, even though taste, etc., for the Sages did not decree on pat akum regarding taste like the substance itself — so too Rashi; but regarding crumbs that are visible, they are certainly forbidden as long as they were not nullified in the majority.`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `And it is not called pat, etc. See Be'er Heitev, Yoreh De'ah part, siman 252.`,
    '2#_': `Even if it is kneaded with eggs. Avodat HaTaharah; and see in the responsum of Devar Yosef, siman 28: in places where they practiced leniency regarding pat akum we have no power to forbid buying a roll from the gentile baker who also sells cakes kneaded with water and forbidden fat, for the baker has special vessels for each craft as is the way of artisans; and all the more so that he himself showed them that he has vessels, a mold, and a pan dedicated whether for kneading, shaping, or baking, and he certainly would not lie to ruin his craft. Also there is a double doubt: perhaps they used today a vessel that absorbed forbidden taste, or perhaps the rolls were baked — perhaps they did not use it; and if you say they used it, perhaps in something that spoils the taste, etc.; and ordinarily a shopkeeper wipes his vessels, etc.; and one need not be concerned lest he take with soiled hands or lest some mixture occur unintentionally — regarding this the Raavad did not say we follow the majority of gentiles, etc. — see there. But it is good to distance oneself from ugliness; and in the responsa of Mayim Rabim, Yoreh De'ah part, many responsa forbid and permit, and he writes that those who forbid were counted and outnumbered those who permit — see there at length; and so too Be'er Heitev, siman 253.`,
    '3#_': `Or kinds of sweets. Avodat HaTaharah; and in Teshuvot Beit Tzvi, volume 3, siman 11, that one should forbid the sufganiyot because of bishulei akum — see there.`,
  },
};
